;;; A `:where` keyword on a rule is ignored, so the rule fires without its
;;; guard — regression `where-on-rule`
;;; (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two `p` facts and a rule that writes `:where (neq ?x a)` as a keyword of
;;; the rule form, after its `:assert`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A rule takes a closed list of keywords, and `:where` is not on it
;;; (Q-M1e2.28), so the program is refused at the parse, at the keyword:
;;; every command that reads it exits 1 (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `ec004a48` (the engine `916d97ac`'s), release and debug alike: the
;;; keyword was ignored, so `r` fired for every `p`: root derived `(q a)` and
;;; `(q b)`, with no line (`ein saturate FILE --dump`), and R was four states
;;; over the candidates `(hit a)` and `(hit b)`, exit 0 (`ein solve FILE`,
;;; `ein oracle FILE`).
;;;
;;;
;;;

(relation p Node) (relation q Node) (relation hit Node)
(p a) (p b)
(rule r () :match (p ?x) :assert (q ?x) :where (neq ?x a))
